    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>During systemic fasting or hypoglycemia, pancreatic &alpha;-cells secrete the 29-amino-acid peptide hormone glucagon to stimulate hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis while suppressing glycolysis and glycogenesis. Glucagon initiates hepatic signaling by binding to the extracellular domain of the glucagon receptor (GCGR), a class B secretin-like seven-transmembrane G protein-coupled receptor. Agonist occupancy induces a conformational rearrangement in the cytoplasmic loops of GCGR, catalyzing guanosine diphosphate (GDP) dissociation and guanosine triphosphate (GTP) binding on the heterotrimeric G<sub>&alpha;s</sub> subunit.</p>\n<p>Activated G<sub>&alpha;s</sub>-GTP dissociates from the G<sub>&beta;&gamma;</sub> heterodimer and allosterically activates transmembrane adenylyl cyclases (AC, primarily AC6 and AC9). Adenylyl cyclase catalyzes the cyclization of ATP into adenosine 3',5'-cyclic monophosphate (cAMP) with concomitant release of inorganic pyrophosphate (PP<sub>i</sub>). Cyclic AMP functions as an allosteric second messenger that binds with high affinity to two tandem cyclic nucleotide-binding domains on each regulatory (R) subunit of the inactive protein kinase A holoenzyme tetramer (R<sub>2</sub>C<sub>2</sub>). Cooperative binding of four cAMP molecules causes conformational dissociation of the regulatory subunits, unmasking the catalytic active sites of the two free C subunits.</p>\n<p>Active PKA catalytic subunits phosphorylate specific serine and threonine residues on key regulatory metabolic enzymes:\n1. Phosphorylation of the tandem bifunctional enzyme 6-phosphofructo-2-kinase / fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase-1 (PFK-2/FBPase-2) at Ser32 inactivates the kinase domain and activates the bisphosphatase domain, precipitating an acute fall in cellular fructose 2,6-bisphosphate (F-2,6-P<sub>2</sub>).\n2. Phosphorylation of liver-type pyruvate kinase (L-PK) at Ser515, reducing its catalytic affinity for phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP).\n3. Phosphorylation and activation of phosphorylase kinase, which phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase <em>b</em> into active <em>a</em>, driving glycogenolysis.\n4. Phosphorylation of glycogen synthase at multiple serine residues, converting active <em>a</em> to inactive <em>b</em>.</p>\n<p>Primary rat hepatocytes were exposed to vehicle, glucagon (10 nM), forskolin (an adenylyl cyclase diterpene activator), the PKA competitive catalytic inhibitor H-89, or the selective exchange protein directly activated by cAMP (Epac) agonist 8-pCPT-2'-O-Me-cAMP. Intracellular cAMP, [F-2,6-P<sub>2</sub>], glycogen phosphorylase <em>a</em> activity, and net glucose release were evaluated (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Second messenger levels, F-2,6-P<sub>2</sub> concentrations, phosphorylase activity, and glucose release across hepatocyte treatments</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Hepatocyte Treatment</th><th>Intracellular cAMP (pmol/mg)</th><th>Fructose 2,6-Bisphosphate (nmol/g)</th><th>Phosphorylase <em>a</em> Activity (% Total)</th><th>Net Glucose Release (&mu;mol/h&middot;g cell)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Vehicle Control</td><td>1.8 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>9.4 &plusmn; 0.6</td><td>14.2 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>18.5 &plusmn; 1.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Glucagon (10 nM)</td><td>16.5 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>82.6 &plusmn; 4.5</td><td>114.2 &plusmn; 6.8</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Glucagon + H-89</td><td>15.9 &plusmn; 1.0</td><td>8.9 &plusmn; 0.5</td><td>18.4 &plusmn; 1.3</td><td>24.1 &plusmn; 1.9</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Forskolin (10 &mu;M)</td><td>22.4 &plusmn; 1.4</td><td>1.0 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>85.1 &plusmn; 4.8</td><td>121.6 &plusmn; 7.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Forskolin + H-89</td><td>21.8 &plusmn; 1.3</td><td>8.6 &plusmn; 0.6</td><td>19.1 &plusmn; 1.4</td><td>26.5 &plusmn; 2.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Epac Agonist (50 &mu;M)</td><td>2.0 &plusmn; 0.2</td><td>9.1 &plusmn; 0.7</td><td>15.8 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>21.0 &plusmn; 1.6</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
