        "e": "<strong>Selective beta-2 adrenergic antagonism in respiratory airways (Topic 596):</strong> Bronchial smooth muscle expresses a high density of &beta;<sub>2</sub>-adrenergic receptors. Epinephrine is a potent non-selective adrenergic agonist; when applied to airway rings, it binds &beta;<sub>2</sub>-AR, surging cAMP to 21.8 pmol/mg, which activates PKA, reduces phospho-MLCK to 42%, and drops mechanical force to 8% (bronchodilation). The drug **ICI 118,551** is a potent, highly selective competitive antagonist at &beta;<sub>2</sub>-adrenergic receptors. By competitively occupying the &beta;<sub>2</sub>-AR ligand-binding pocket, ICI 118,551 prevents epinephrine from activating G<sub>&alpha;s</sub> and adenylyl cyclase (cAMP drops to 3.5 pmol/mg in Table 1). In the absence of PKA-mediated inhibition, baseline contractile tone is restored (phospho-MLCK returns to 97% and mechanical force reaches 88%). This illustrates why non-selective &beta;-blockers (e.g., propranolol) are contraindicated in asthma, as blocking &beta;<sub>2</sub>-AR precipitates life-threatening bronchospasm.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is incorrect because ICI 118,551 is a &beta;<sub>2</sub> antagonist, not an &alpha;<sub>1</sub> blocker, and blocking &alpha;<sub>1</sub> would decrease G<sub>&alpha;q</sub> signaling rather than shifting toward it.<br>&bull; C describes PDE4 stimulation; however, PDE4 hydrolyzes cAMP (not IP<sub>3</sub>), and ICI 118,551 is a receptor antagonist, not an enzyme activator.<br>&bull; D describes SERCA2a inhibition, which is not the pharmacological target of &beta;-blockers."
