        "a": 1,
        "e": "<strong>Karyopherin-mediated nuclear transport of homodimerized GR (Topic 597):</strong> Transport of large proteins (> 40 kDa) across the nuclear envelope occurs through nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) and requires soluble karyopherin transport receptors. Upon cortisol binding and chaperone release, homodimerized GR unmasks two nuclear localization signals (NLSs): NLS1 (a classic basic monopartite/bipartite motif adjacent to the DBD) and NLS2 within the LBD. The adaptor protein **importin-&alpha;1** (karyopherin-&alpha;1 / *KPNA2*) recognizes and binds the exposed basic NLS of GR. Importin-&alpha; then recruits **importin-&beta;**, forming a heterotrimeric transport complex. Importin-&beta; interacts transiently with hydrophobic phenylalanine-glycine (FG) repeat nucleoporins lining the central aqueous pore of the NPC, ferrying the GR cargo into the nucleus in a RanGTP-regulated manner. In hepatocytes depleted of importin-&alpha;1 via siRNA, liganded GR cannot engage the nuclear transport machinery and remains trapped in the cytoplasm (translocation drops to 14.8% in row 6). Without nuclear entry, GR cannot occupy the *PCK1* promoter GRE (ChIP falls to 1.5-fold), blunting *PCK1* transcription and gluconeogenesis.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A confuses a nuclear transport adaptor with lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), which oxidizes lactate to pyruvate in the cytoplasm.<br>&bull; C describes a cytoplasmic corepressor/chaperone, but importin-&alpha;1 is a nuclear import karyopherin.<br>&bull; D is incorrect because transcription is executed by RNA polymerase II along with transcription factors, not importin transport adaptors."
