        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Enzymatic mechanism and stoichiometry of PEPCK (Topic 597):</strong> In gluconeogenesis, the irreversible glycolytic reaction catalyzed by pyruvate kinase (conversion of PEP to pyruvate and ATP) is bypassed by a coordinated two-step enzymatic detour: (1) In mitochondria, **pyruvate carboxylase** carboxylates pyruvate to oxaloacetate (OAA) consuming ATP, $CO_2$, and a biotin cofactor. (2) Because OAA cannot directly cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, it is shuttled to the cytosol via the malate-aspartate shuttle. In the cytosol, **phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK / *PCK1*)** catalyzes the irreversible **decarboxylation and phosphorylation** of oxaloacetate: $\\text{Oxaloacetate} + \\text{GTP} \\rightarrow \\text{Phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP)} + \\text{GDP} + \\text{CO}_2$. The free energy released by decarboxylation (loss of $CO_2$) provides the thermodynamic driving force to couple the transfer of a high-energy phosphate group from GTP onto the enol oxygen, forming the high-energy enol-phosphate bond of phosphoenolpyruvate.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH), which converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, entering the citric acid cycle.<br>&bull; B describes pyruvate carboxylase, the mitochondrial enzyme that catalyzes the first step of the bypass.<br>&bull; C describes pyruvate kinase, the glycolytic enzyme that runs in the reverse direction during glycolysis."
