        "e": "<strong>Mechanism of glucocorticoid trans-repression (Topic 597):</strong> The anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive actions of cortisol and synthetic glucocorticoids (e.g., dexamethasone, prednisone) occur predominantly through **trans-repression**, a mechanism distinct from classical GRE-mediated transcriptional trans-activation: In trans-repression, ligand-bound **monomeric GR** physically associates via direct protein-protein interactions with the p65 (RelA) subunit of nuclear factor-&kappa;B (**NF-&kappa;B**) and the c-Jun subunit of activator protein 1 (**AP-1**). NF-&kappa;B and AP-1 are the master transcriptional drivers of pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1&beta;, TNF-&alpha;, IL-6, IL-8), cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), and inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS). Physical tethering of GR to NF-&kappa;B/AP-1 prevents their association with the basal transcription machinery, recruits histone deacetylases (HDAC2) to condense promoter chromatin, and blocks cytokine gene transcription without requiring GR to bind GRE DNA sequences directly.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Directional Inversion trap; glucocorticoids repress IL-1&beta; and inflammatory cytokines rather than stimulating them.<br>&bull; B describes mitochondrial apoptosis mechanisms, which do not mediate anti-inflammatory trans-repression.<br>&bull; D describes extracellular proteolysis; GR is an intracellular transcription factor, not an extracellular protease, and prostaglandins are eicosanoids (lipids), not proteins."
