        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Chemical structure and backbone of ceramide (Topic 600):</strong> Unlike glycerophospholipids (which are built upon a 3-carbon glycerol backbone esterified to fatty acids), all sphingolipids are constructed upon **ceramide**. Ceramide consists of **sphingosine** (trans-1,3-dihydroxy-2-amino-4-octadecene, an 18-carbon amino alcohol possessing an unsaturated hydrocarbon chain, two hydroxyl groups at C-1 and C-3, and an amino group at C-2) joined to a long-chain fatty acid via an **amide bond** at the C-2 amino group. The terminal primary hydroxyl group at C-1 serves as the attachment site for polar headgroups: attachment of phosphocholine yields sphingomyelin, whereas attachment of carbohydrate sugars yields glycosphingolipids (cerebrosides and gangliosides).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes a triacylglycerol (triglyceride), composed of a glycerol backbone esterified to three fatty acids.<br>&bull; B describes cholesterol and steroid derivatives (four fused hydrocarbon rings: cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene).<br>&bull; C describes phosphoinositides / phytic acid derivatives, which possess a phosphorylated inositol ring rather than a sphingoid base."
