        "e": "<strong>Biosynthesis of sphingomyelin by sphingomyelin synthase (Topic 600):</strong> Sphingomyelin is unique among sphingolipids because it is both a sphingolipid and a phospholipid (containing a phosphodiester linkage). Following de novo ceramide synthesis in the ER, ceramide is transported to the Golgi apparatus by the ceramide transfer protein (CERT). In the lumenal leaflet of the trans-Golgi, **sphingomyelin synthase (SMS)** transfers the **phosphocholine headgroup from phosphatidylcholine (lecithin)** onto the primary C-1 hydroxyl group of ceramide. This enzymatic transfer yields **sphingomyelin** and concomitantly generates **1,2-diacylglycerol (DAG)**: $\\text{Ceramide} + \\text{Phosphatidylcholine} \\rightarrow \\text{Sphingomyelin} + 1,2\\text{-Diacylglycerol}$. The co-produced DAG modulates protein kinase C signaling and Golgi vesicular trafficking.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes galactosylceramide synthesis by galactosylceramide synthase using UDP-galactose, generating cerebrosides rather than sphingomyelin.<br>&bull; B describes ceramide kinase, which phosphorylates ceramide using ATP to form ceramide-1-phosphate (C1P), an inflammatory signaling lipid.<br>&bull; D describes the Kennedy pathway for phosphatidylethanolamine synthesis (utilizing CDP-ethanolamine), which does not participate in sphingomyelin formation."
