        "e": "<strong>Genetics and quaternary structure of beta-hexosaminidases A and B (Topic 601):</strong> Lysosomal &beta;-hexosaminidases exist as dimeric isoenzymes formed by combinations of two distinct homologous subunits: the &alpha;-subunit (encoded by the **<em>HEXA</em>** gene on chromosome 15) and the &beta;-subunit (encoded by the **<em>HEXB</em>** gene on chromosome 5). (1) **Hexosaminidase A (Hex A)** is an **&alpha;&beta; heterodimer**. Only the &alpha;-subunit contains the active site capable of hydrolyzing negatively charged substrates when presented by the GM2 activator protein. Therefore, mutations in *HEXA* eliminate Hex A activity, causing **Tay-Sachs disease**, where GM2 gangliosides accumulate while neutral globosides are cleared normally by Hex B. (2) **Hexosaminidase B (Hex B)** is a **&beta;&beta; homodimer**. Mutations in *HEXB* (chromosome 5) cause **Sandhoff disease**, disrupting both Hex B (&beta;&beta;) and Hex A (&alpha;&beta;). Consequently, Sandhoff disease features accumulation of both GM2 gangliosides and globosides, presenting with a severe Tay-Sachs-like neurodegeneration alongside visceral organomegaly.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A confuses glycoside hydrolases with proteases and kinases; both Hex A and Hex B are lysosomal acid glycosidases.<br>&bull; B confuses hexosaminidases with glucocerebrosidase and sphingomyelinase.<br>&bull; C is incorrect because both Hex A and Hex B are targeted to the acidic lumen of lysosomes via the mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) pathway."
