        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Glutathione conjugation in cysteinyl leukotriene biosynthesis (Topic 605):</strong> The cysteinyl leukotrienes (originally designated the 'slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis' [SRS-A]) are distinguished by the presence of a sulfur-linked peptide at carbon-6 of the eicosanoid chain. The branch-point intermediate **leukotriene A<sub>4</sub> (LTA<sub>4</sub>)** contains an unstable 5,6-epoxide ring. The integral nuclear membrane enzyme **leukotriene C<sub>4</sub> synthase (LTC4S)** couples LTA<sub>4</sub> with the reduced intracellular antioxidant tripeptide **glutathione (&gamma;-L-glutamyl-L-cysteinylglycine / GSH)**. The sulfhydryl group of glutathione's cysteine residue attacks the epoxide ring, opening it to form **leukotriene C<sub>4</sub> (LTC<sub>4</sub>)** via a stable thioether linkage. Extracellular peptidases subsequently trim the peptide chain: &gamma;-glutamyl leukotrienase cleaves the glutamic acid residue to yield **leukotriene D<sub>4</sub> (LTD<sub>4</sub>)**, and dipeptidases then remove the glycine residue to yield **leukotriene E<sub>4</sub> (LTE<sub>4</sub>)**.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes direct addition of free cysteine, but free cysteine is never added directly; the cysteinyl moiety is derived from the complete glutathione tripeptide and subsequent sequential peptidase trimming.<br>&bull; B describes carnosine, an antioxidant dipeptide found in skeletal muscle, not leukotriene synthesis.<br>&bull; C describes SAM, the methyl donor in transmethylation reactions, not thioether lipid conjugation."
