        "a": 1,
        "e": "<strong>AMPK energy sensing and metabolic reprogramming (Topic 593):</strong> AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) is the master eukaryotic cellular energy sensor, activated by an elevated AMP/ATP or ADP/ATP ratio as well as by the adipokine adiponectin via AdipoR1/2 receptors and the upstream kinase LKB1. Once activated by phosphorylation at Thr172, AMPK coordinates a comprehensive metabolic switch: it **shuts off ATP-consuming anabolic pathways** and **turns on ATP-generating catabolic pathways**. In lipid metabolism, AMPK directly phosphorylates and inactivates **acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC)**. Inactivation of ACC drops the concentration of **malonyl-CoA**, the first committed intermediate of fatty acid synthesis. Because malonyl-CoA is the physiological allosteric inhibitor of carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1 (CPT-1), a fall in malonyl-CoA disinhibits CPT-1, allowing fatty acyl-CoAs to enter mitochondria for accelerated $\\beta$-oxidation and ATP regeneration. Simultaneously, AMPK phosphorylates and inhibits HMG-CoA reductase (halting cholesterol synthesis), inhibits glycogen synthase, and phosphorylates TSC2 and Raptor to shut down mTORC1-mediated protein synthesis.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A displays complete Directional Inversion; AMPK phosphorylates and *inactivates* ACC, suppressing fatty acid synthesis rather than accelerating it.<br>&bull; C displays Directional Inversion; AMPK *inhibits* glycogen synthase and *inhibits* mTORC1 to preserve ATP rather than promoting energy-expensive translation.<br>&bull; D displays Directional Inversion; AMPK phosphorylates HMG-CoA reductase at Ser872 to *inactivate* it and shut down mevalonate/cholesterol biosynthesis."
