        "e": "<strong>Genomic mechanism of thyroid hormone calorigenesis (Topic 598):</strong> Thyroid hormones\u2014predominantly **triiodothyronine (T<sub>3</sub>)**, generated peripherally from thyroxine ($T_4$) by 5'-deiodinases\u2014are lipophilic amine hormones that enter target cells via monocarboxylate transporters (MCT8/10) to engage nuclear thyroid hormone receptors (TR&alpha; and TR&beta;). TRs bind as heterodimers with the retinoid X receptor (RXR) to thyroid response elements (TREs) on target gene promoters. Upon $T_3$ binding, TR recruits histone acetyltransferases to activate transcription of critical metabolic genes: (1) **$\text{Na}^+/\text{K}^+$-ATPase:** $T_3$ upregulates transcription of both &alpha; and &beta; subunits of the sodium pump across skeletal muscle, liver, and kidney. Pumping ions against gradients consumes up to 30&ndash;40% of baseline cellular ATP, accelerating ADP generation, mitochondrial electron transport, and oxygen consumption. (2) **Mitochondrial biogenesis and uncoupling proteins:** $T_3$ induces PGC-1&alpha; and uncoupling proteins (UCP-1, UCP-3), dissipating the electrochemical proton gradient as heat. Together, these genomic actions account for the hallmark elevation in basal metabolic rate (BMR), thermogenesis, and cellular respiration seen in hyperthyroidism.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Receptor Misattribution trap; thyroid hormones act predominantly via nuclear transcription factor receptors (TR/RXR), not cell-surface G protein-coupled receptors.<br>&bull; B describes direct kinase phosphorylation; $T_3$ is a hormone, not an enzymatic protein kinase, and increases respiratory capacity via transcriptional induction rather than direct phosphorylation of Complex IV.<br>&bull; D describes potassium channel modulation, which does not mediate the sustained calorigenic transcription program driven by $T_3$."
