        "e": "<strong>The GH / IGF-1 somatotropic anabolic axis (Topic 599):</strong> Somatotropin (growth hormone, GH) is a 191-amino-acid peptide secreted by anterior pituitary somatotrophs under the stimulatory control of hypothalamic GHRH and inhibitory tone of somatostatin. GH binds to dimeric cytokine-family growth hormone receptors (GHR) on hepatocytes, activating JAK2 and STAT5 to induce transcription and systemic endocrine secretion of **insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1 / somatomedin C)**. In the epiphyseal plates of growing long bones, GH also stimulates local chondrocyte differentiation. Circulating and paracrine IGF-1 binds to the IGF-1 receptor (IGF-1R), a receptor tyrosine kinase structurally related to the insulin receptor. Autophosphorylation of IGF-1R triggers the PI3K-Akt and MAPK signaling cascades in chondrocytes, stimulating DNA replication, clonal proliferation, amino acid uptake, and synthesis of extracellular cartilage matrix (collagen type II and proteoglycans). Subsequent hypertrophic chondrocyte differentiation, vascular invasion, and osteoblast recruitment drive endochondral ossification and longitudinal skeletal lengthening.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A displays Directional Inversion; GH powerfully *stimulates* hepatic IGF-1 secretion, and stimulates bone formation rather than activating osteoclastic bone resorption.<br>&bull; B is a Receptor Misattribution trap; GH is a peptide hormone that binds cell-surface transmembrane cytokine receptors (GHR), not intracellular nuclear steroid receptors.<br>&bull; C is false because IGF-1 stimulates chondrocyte nutrient uptake and protein synthesis rather than downregulating it."
