        "e": "<strong>Pathophysiology and genetics of Fabry disease (Topic 604):</strong> Fabry disease is unique among the classic sphingolipidoses in being inherited in an **X-linked recessive** pattern (all others, such as Tay-Sachs, Gaucher, Niemann-Pick, and Krabbe, are autosomal recessive). It is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the **<em>GLA</em>** gene, which encodes the lysosomal acid hydrolase **&alpha;-galactosidase A**. Deficiency of &alpha;-galactosidase A impairs the cleavage of terminal &alpha;-D-galactosyl residues, leading to progressive lysosomal accumulation of **globotriaosylceramide (Gb<sub>3</sub> / ceramide trihexoside)** within vascular endothelial cells, vascular smooth muscle, renal podocytes, and dorsal root ganglion neurons. Early childhood/adolescent manifestations include episodic, excruciating burning pain in the extremities (**acroparesthesias**), hypohidrosis (inability to sweat), and clusterings of non-blanching, dark red-purple punctate macules and papules on the groin and lower abdomen (**angiokeratomas**). Over decades, microvascular endothelial Gb<sub>3</sub> deposition causes progressive glomerulosclerosis with proteinuria and renal failure, cardiac hypertrophy, and premature stroke. Electron microscopy of renal biopsies classically demonstrates diagnostic **'zebra bodies'** (lamellar cytoplasmic glycolipid inclusions).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD), caused by arylsulfatase A deficiency resulting in sulfatide accumulation, presenting with central and peripheral demyelination, ataxia, and dementia, without angiokeratomas.<br>&bull; C describes Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis Type I), caused by &alpha;-L-iduronidase deficiency causing heparan and dermatan sulfate accumulation, presenting with gargoylism, corneal clouding, and hepatosplenomegaly.<br>&bull; D describes Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy), caused by galactocerebrosidase deficiency accumulating galactosylceramide and psychosine."
