        "e": "<strong>Division of labor at the eukaryotic replication fork (Topic 606):</strong> Eukaryotic genomic replication requires the highly coordinated division of labor among three distinct B-family DNA polymerases: (1) **DNA Polymerase &alpha; / Primase complex:** Initiates synthesis at replication origins and the beginning of each Okazaki fragment. The primase subunit synthesizes a short RNA primer (&approx;10 nucleotides), which Pol &alpha; extends with &approx;20 deoxyribonucleotides of DNA (initiator DNA / iDNA). Pol &alpha; lacks $3' \\rightarrow 5'$ exonuclease proofreading activity. (2) **DNA Polymerase &epsilon; (Pol &epsilon;):** Following a polymerase switch mediated by replication factor C (RFC) loading proliferating cell nuclear antigen (PCNA), Pol &epsilon; takes over synthesis of the **continuous leading strand**. Pol &epsilon; possesses high processivity and intrinsic $3' \\rightarrow 5'$ exonuclease proofreading activity, synthesizing millions of base pairs without dissociating. (3) **DNA Polymerase &delta; (Pol &delta;):** Associates with the homotrimeric PCNA sliding clamp to execute high-fidelity, proofread synthesis of the **discontinuous lagging strand** (Okazaki fragments). Pol &delta; also coordinates with flap endonuclease 1 (FEN1) and DNA ligase I to process and seal Okazaki fragments.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A inverts the roles of Pol &alpha; and Pol &delta;; Pol &alpha; synthesizes the initial hybrid primers, while Pol &delta; replicates the lagging strand.<br>&bull; B describes primer removal and ligation; RNA primers are removed by FEN1 / RNase H, and nicks are sealed by DNA ligase I, not polymerases.<br>&bull; D is incorrect because Pol &alpha; lacks proofreading activity and Pol &delta; does not synthesize RNA primers."
